From 0c11cdecc5204b5d37617e55311e932a6c4641f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 17:02:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 69/74] CPU monitor: LEARN + tempo encoder press switches it
 off and on, and its mode survives a restart

Settings > CPU monitor was off after every power-up and only reachable through the menu. Now:

- Shortcut: LEARN held and the tempo encoder pressed (hid/buttons.cpp, before the view gets the button, so it works in
  every view, playing or stopped; with Shift the press still clears the tempo automation). It switches between Off and
  the last of On and Alerts (at first On); from Profile it goes to Off (a profiler measurement is chosen in the menu).
  Popup "CPU monitor: On/Off/Alerts" on the OLED, "C-ON", "COFF", "C-AL" on the 7-segment display.
- Saved: a hidden entry cpuMonitor in CommunityFeatures.XML, written and read like masterTune (no new
  RuntimeFeatureSettingType, no flash byte); firmware that doesn't know it keeps it as an unknown setting. The shortcut
  saves at once; while the card is busy (sdRoutineLock) it leaves the save to cpu_stats::routine(), which does it once
  the card is free. The menu saves it when left, as all settings. At boot readSettingsFromFile() sets the mode; a saved
  Profile starts as On (USB isn't open yet), no entry leaves it Off.

Unlike the design, the press is not deferred with REMIND_ME_OUTSIDE_CARD_ROUTINE in the card routine: inCardRoutine is
sdRoutineLock (deluge.cpp), so the pending save would never be reached, and the press would hold up all later input
until the SD routine ends. Switching the mode only sets flags and shows a popup (as the tempo popup does there).

Test: mastertune tests/settings/cpu_monitor_shortcut_emu.py (four boots on one card: Song view stopped, clip view
playing, the card busy, restart with the OLED line and SysEx, the menu on Alerts and Profile, keyboard and drone view):
30 of 30; the v16 release: 8 of 30 (the feature's checks fail). usb_audio_setting_emu.py 2 of 2, profiler_emu.py 21 of
21, tests/song/run.sh (--init-sounds --seed 1): demand 87a7df292189e341, digital 4473b3155dcfa0e8, as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
